WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.030 --> 00:00:07.770
My name is Travis
Gerke, and I'm

00:00:07.770 --> 00:00:12.060
the director of data science
at PCCTC, a clinical research

00:00:12.060 --> 00:00:15.480
organization facilitating
clinical trials in the cancer

00:00:15.480 --> 00:00:16.900
space.

00:00:16.900 --> 00:00:18.520
I used to be an
academic and when

00:00:18.520 --> 00:00:20.350
I was transitioning
to industry roles,

00:00:20.350 --> 00:00:23.170
I found challenges in
refactoring my resume

00:00:23.170 --> 00:00:27.150
and professional portfolio to
align with industry standards.

00:00:27.150 --> 00:00:28.900
I hope to share some
resources that you'll

00:00:28.900 --> 00:00:30.450
find useful in this talk,

00:00:30.450 --> 00:00:33.040
What they forgot to teach you
about industry transitions

00:00:33.040 --> 00:00:39.962
from academia. Or, more
simply, WTF AITA.

00:00:39.962 --> 00:00:41.920
If you're not familiar
with these two acronyms,

00:00:41.920 --> 00:00:44.460
we'll consider each in turn.

00:00:44.460 --> 00:00:46.710
First, if in the
context of your job

00:00:46.710 --> 00:00:48.600
you've ever asked what the [bleep]?

00:00:48.600 --> 00:00:51.510
I'd probably respond
with great question,

00:00:51.510 --> 00:00:56.110
particularly if you
are an academic.

00:00:56.110 --> 00:00:58.330
This plot shows
one aspect of what

00:00:58.330 --> 00:01:01.720
the [bleep]-ery in academia, the
increasing number of PhDs

00:01:01.720 --> 00:01:04.930
awarded versus the much
smaller number of faculty jobs

00:01:04.930 --> 00:01:06.040
available.

00:01:06.040 --> 00:01:08.050
The gap between these
two diverging numbers

00:01:08.050 --> 00:01:12.620
has helpfully 
been labeled WTF.

00:01:12.620 --> 00:01:14.190
There are lots of other reasons.

00:01:14.190 --> 00:01:15.470
One might ask what the [bleep]

00:01:15.470 --> 00:01:16.440
in academia,

00:01:16.440 --> 00:01:18.920
but this is not really
a talk about that.

00:01:18.920 --> 00:01:21.710
I would instead refer you
to the so-called quit lit

00:01:21.710 --> 00:01:24.320
where people have discussed
their thoughts and experiences

00:01:24.320 --> 00:01:27.830
on leaving academia in a variety
of formats, such as blogs

00:01:27.830 --> 00:01:29.720
and social media posts.

00:01:29.720 --> 00:01:32.660
Indeed, academia can be
a highly rewarding career

00:01:32.660 --> 00:01:34.430
if it's what you
want, and this talk

00:01:34.430 --> 00:01:38.030
is not at all focused on
criticizing that career choice.

00:01:38.030 --> 00:01:38.910
All the same,

00:01:38.910 --> 00:01:41.270
many academics are
currently industry curious,

00:01:41.270 --> 00:01:43.700
and I hope that many
who are not in academia

00:01:43.700 --> 00:01:46.190
will still find useful
content in this talk.

00:01:46.190 --> 00:01:48.020
Academics certainly
don't hold the monopoly

00:01:48.020 --> 00:01:51.110
on asking what the [bleep].

00:01:51.110 --> 00:01:54.300
The second acronym,
AITA, stands for.

00:01:54.300 --> 00:01:56.090
Am I the asshole?

00:01:56.090 --> 00:01:57.830
This happens a lot
when academics first

00:01:57.830 --> 00:01:59.750
apply to industry roles.

00:01:59.750 --> 00:02:02.460
As a specific example, I
was asking this question

00:02:02.460 --> 00:02:05.450
when my first job
applications to industry roles

00:02:05.450 --> 00:02:07.080
received no response.

00:02:07.080 --> 00:02:09.350
I thought, am I not qualified?

00:02:09.350 --> 00:02:10.669
Are they the asshole?

00:02:10.669 --> 00:02:13.080
Am I the asshole?

00:02:13.080 --> 00:02:15.270
In this talk, we'll focus
on three common ways

00:02:15.270 --> 00:02:17.380
that academics can
unintentionally be the hole.

00:02:17.380 --> 00:02:22.170
The first, resumes that are
way too long are common.

00:02:22.170 --> 00:02:24.780
Second, cover letters
should not be

00:02:24.780 --> 00:02:27.030
a thesis on the direction
of your research,

00:02:27.030 --> 00:02:30.000
rather a clear and focused
statement of your value

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:33.280
that is aesthetically
streamlined. And last,

00:02:33.280 --> 00:02:36.300
an untidy coding portfolio
can make it very difficult

00:02:36.300 --> 00:02:38.580
to see how the first
two items come together

00:02:38.580 --> 00:02:41.190
to make you amazing.

00:02:41.190 --> 00:02:43.740
In short, avoiding
[bleep]hole-ery really

00:02:43.740 --> 00:02:46.020
means not asking others
to work hard to discover

00:02:46.020 --> 00:02:48.050
how awesome you really are.

00:02:48.050 --> 00:02:50.870
As a current reviewer of
many application packages,

00:02:50.870 --> 00:02:53.420
I see some truly excellent,
excellent candidates

00:02:53.420 --> 00:02:56.570
who could shine that much
brighter with some finesse.

00:02:56.570 --> 00:02:59.030
Fortunately, the R
community has created

00:02:59.030 --> 00:03:04.400
resources which make each of the
three problems easily solvable.

00:03:04.400 --> 00:03:06.295
First, let's look at resumes.

00:03:08.990 --> 00:03:11.900
Here, quite embarrassingly,
is the 22 page

00:03:11.900 --> 00:03:15.590
resume that I sent as it
is to the first industry

00:03:15.590 --> 00:03:17.990
roles to which I applied.

00:03:17.990 --> 00:03:20.240
This beast served
me well in academia

00:03:20.240 --> 00:03:21.980
where CVs are required
to be formatted

00:03:21.980 --> 00:03:23.430
like this. In industry,

00:03:23.430 --> 00:03:28.060
however, yes, I was the asshole.

00:03:28.060 --> 00:03:30.310
Here, on the other hand,
is a stellar resume

00:03:30.310 --> 00:03:33.130
that I received about a year ago
from someone a lot of you probably

00:03:33.130 --> 00:03:34.490
recognize this at this conf.

00:03:34.490 --> 00:03:36.130
Shannon Pileggi.

00:03:36.130 --> 00:03:38.920
This elegant, elegant
two pager gives me

00:03:38.920 --> 00:03:42.400
an easily accessible view of
Shannon's skills, experience

00:03:42.400 --> 00:03:44.890
and clear links to
her portfolio, a topic

00:03:44.890 --> 00:03:46.740
that we'll discuss later.

00:03:46.740 --> 00:03:48.510
I'm lucky enough to
work with Shannon now,

00:03:48.510 --> 00:03:52.170
and she gave me permission
to use this as an example.

00:03:52.170 --> 00:03:54.500
The same goes for any examples
I'll show in this talk.

00:03:54.500 --> 00:03:56.700
I either have permission
from the content owner

00:03:56.700 --> 00:03:58.590
or it's publicly available.

00:03:58.590 --> 00:04:00.970
If you want your resume
to look like Shannon's,

00:04:00.970 --> 00:04:03.630
fortunately, there is an
R package for that.

00:04:03.630 --> 00:04:06.030
It's called datadrivencv.

00:04:06.030 --> 00:04:08.310
I won't repeat any
of the how to content

00:04:08.310 --> 00:04:09.840
that the package
author Nick Strayer

00:04:09.840 --> 00:04:11.400
has carefully assembled.

00:04:11.400 --> 00:04:13.020
If you go to the
package vignette,

00:04:13.020 --> 00:04:15.870
you should be up and
running in under an hour.

00:04:15.870 --> 00:04:18.300
Aside from making a resume
that looks really slick,

00:04:18.300 --> 00:04:20.490
there's a big bonus with
using a package like this

00:04:20.490 --> 00:04:21.810
that I want to mention.

00:04:21.810 --> 00:04:23.640
When hiring managers
like myself get

00:04:23.640 --> 00:04:27.450
a stack of 50 to 100 resumes,
I can recognize right away

00:04:27.450 --> 00:04:30.960
those applicants that use R
to create their materials.

00:04:30.960 --> 00:04:32.640
This practice sends
a signal that you're

00:04:32.640 --> 00:04:35.460
excited about integrating
R based workflows into all

00:04:35.460 --> 00:04:37.800
of your activities, not
just those that involve

00:04:37.800 --> 00:04:39.880
statistics or data analysis.

00:04:39.880 --> 00:04:43.893
And it's a sure thing that I'll
read those resumes carefully.

00:04:43.893 --> 00:04:45.810
Something else that the
datadrivencv package

00:04:45.810 --> 00:04:47.700
does that's nice
is that it makes

00:04:47.700 --> 00:04:49.980
it easy to include
or omit information

00:04:49.980 --> 00:04:52.740
for long or short
versions of your resume.

00:04:52.740 --> 00:04:56.250
For instance, if you still
need a long academic variation

00:04:56.250 --> 00:04:58.230
for some purposes,
you can render

00:04:58.230 --> 00:05:01.303
with all the publications and
fluff into dozens of pages.

00:05:01.303 --> 00:05:02.970
And then, if you want
the industry ready

00:05:02.970 --> 00:05:06.150
two pager, a quick filter
within datadrivencv

00:05:06.150 --> 00:05:07.530
will get you there.

00:05:07.530 --> 00:05:09.930
This eases some of
the pain I experienced

00:05:09.930 --> 00:05:12.330
in moving the currency of
academia, pages

00:05:12.330 --> 00:05:15.150
and pages of publications,
to a single bullet

00:05:15.150 --> 00:05:15.995
point in my resume.

00:05:19.340 --> 00:05:22.500
Next, let's discuss
cover letters.

00:05:22.500 --> 00:05:24.870
Much like my
horrific academic CV,

00:05:24.870 --> 00:05:27.660
I was also sending out this
dense research statement

00:05:27.660 --> 00:05:32.350
as a generic cover letter.

00:05:32.350 --> 00:05:34.530
Here, instead, is a template,
which is easy on the eyes

00:05:34.530 --> 00:05:37.920
through generous use of white
space, is succinct at one page,

00:05:37.920 --> 00:05:41.340
and even makes use of bullet
points to highlight key ideas.

00:05:41.340 --> 00:05:43.020
Recall the signal
sending I mentioned

00:05:43.020 --> 00:05:46.770
about resumes clearly built
with R. The same is true here.

00:05:46.770 --> 00:05:49.290
I right away recognize
this as R Markdown

00:05:49.290 --> 00:05:53.220
or LaTeX rendered due to general
font and alignment structure.

00:05:53.220 --> 00:05:55.950
A dead giveaway is the
appearance of AwesomeFont

00:05:55.950 --> 00:05:57.600
icons in the top right.

00:05:57.600 --> 00:05:59.350
That typically doesn't
happen by accident

00:05:59.350 --> 00:06:02.030
in a Word doc.

00:06:02.030 --> 00:06:03.800
If you want to render
letters like this,

00:06:03.800 --> 00:06:07.310
here's the stevetemplates R
package from Steven V Miller.

00:06:07.310 --> 00:06:10.130
Making one of these is as
easy as installing the package

00:06:10.130 --> 00:06:13.490
and creating a new Markdown file
from template in the RStudio

00:06:13.490 --> 00:06:15.720
menu.

00:06:15.720 --> 00:06:17.430
But cover letters
aren't the only thing

00:06:17.430 --> 00:06:20.503
you're allowed to send as part
of your application package.

00:06:20.503 --> 00:06:22.920
Let's have a look at something
really cool in the category

00:06:22.920 --> 00:06:25.710
that I'll call
cover accessories.

00:06:25.710 --> 00:06:27.595
In a recent RStudio
data science hangout,

00:06:27.595 --> 00:06:29.970
which is an awesome community
resource that I'll describe

00:06:29.970 --> 00:06:33.170
later, I met Javier Orraca.

00:06:33.170 --> 00:06:34.820
He described his
recent application

00:06:34.820 --> 00:06:37.730
to bloomreach, in which he
went way above and beyond

00:06:37.730 --> 00:06:41.480
and made a bloomreach Shiny app
with some simulated data that

00:06:41.480 --> 00:06:45.050
is themed with their org's
color scheme and logo.

00:06:45.050 --> 00:06:47.570
The bloomreach team was, of
course, totally blown away

00:06:47.570 --> 00:06:49.850
and Javier landed the job.

00:06:49.850 --> 00:06:51.830
His technique of
demonstrating what's possible

00:06:51.830 --> 00:06:54.230
is Shiny as a business
intelligence tool,

00:06:54.230 --> 00:06:58.190
along with his mastery of
developing in it was spot on.

00:06:58.190 --> 00:07:00.440
If you're interested in
the secret sauce

00:07:00.440 --> 00:07:03.980
here, it's two packages
used inside of Shiny,

00:07:03.980 --> 00:07:05.508
bslib and thematic.

00:07:05.508 --> 00:07:07.550
I'll mention that, if you're
not a Shiny developer

00:07:07.550 --> 00:07:10.040
and the idea of integrating
these components into Shiny

00:07:10.040 --> 00:07:12.050
isn't something you want
to tackle right now,

00:07:12.050 --> 00:07:15.290
don't forget that you can use
these same principles, namely

00:07:15.290 --> 00:07:17.540
organizational
theming and showcasing

00:07:17.540 --> 00:07:20.660
your R skills within a
standard R Markdown document

00:07:20.660 --> 00:07:23.110
that is rendered to HTML or PDF.

00:07:26.860 --> 00:07:30.070
Now, for portfolios.

00:07:30.070 --> 00:07:33.320
Here's a common look for an
academic coding portfolio.

00:07:33.320 --> 00:07:37.030
And yes, this page is
intentionally left blank.

00:07:37.030 --> 00:07:39.580
Academia doesn't incentivize
maintaining this sort of thing,

00:07:39.580 --> 00:07:41.770
but it's hugely
helpful in showcasing

00:07:41.770 --> 00:07:44.110
your technical skills
for industry employers.

00:07:44.110 --> 00:07:46.300
I'm going to show three
levels of portfolios

00:07:46.300 --> 00:07:48.170
with increasing complexity.

00:07:48.170 --> 00:07:50.170
Only one is really
necessary, but it's certainly

00:07:50.170 --> 00:07:51.295
possible to have all three.

00:07:55.660 --> 00:07:58.420
First not only is GitHub
the standard place

00:07:58.420 --> 00:08:01.720
to deposit your projects
and code for public viewing,

00:08:01.720 --> 00:08:04.480
the site also allows you to
put together a personal profile

00:08:04.480 --> 00:08:05.950
on your landing page.

00:08:05.950 --> 00:08:08.140
Here's a great example
for Monica Powell,

00:08:08.140 --> 00:08:11.740
and her link, here, shows you how
to create this using the GitHub

00:08:11.740 --> 00:08:14.350
interface. Here, though,
we're going to see

00:08:14.350 --> 00:08:17.050
how you can do it in RStudio.

00:08:17.050 --> 00:08:19.570
Conveniently, it's just
three lines of code.

00:08:19.570 --> 00:08:21.760
The steps are this,
create an RStudio

00:08:21.760 --> 00:08:26.920
project with the same name as
your GitHub user ID,

00:08:26.920 --> 00:08:30.010
connect it to GitHub, and create
an R Markdown document that

00:08:30.010 --> 00:08:32.380
renders to GitHub
compliant Markdown.

00:08:32.380 --> 00:08:35.320
Again, these three
lines of usethis code

00:08:35.320 --> 00:08:38.200
achieve these three
tasks directly.

00:08:38.200 --> 00:08:40.059
Mash the knit button in RStudio,

00:08:40.059 --> 00:08:41.799
push to GitHub, and you're done.

00:08:41.799 --> 00:08:43.690
Any modifications
you'd like to make

00:08:43.690 --> 00:08:45.430
are just done with
R Markdown syntax

00:08:45.430 --> 00:08:47.378
that you probably already know.

00:08:47.378 --> 00:08:49.420
Standard R plots and
all the bells and whistles

00:08:49.420 --> 00:08:52.140
work here. As a
super basic example,

00:08:52.140 --> 00:08:54.340
here's what happened
when I simply rendered

00:08:54.340 --> 00:08:58.090
the usethis RMD default
document through this method

00:08:58.090 --> 00:09:01.270
and pushed it to my GitHub
repository named my user

00:09:01.270 --> 00:09:03.010
ID, tgerke.

00:09:03.010 --> 00:09:05.012
Cool, right?

00:09:05.012 --> 00:09:06.470
While we're on the
topic of GitHub,

00:09:06.470 --> 00:09:09.050
I want to emphasize that
you should use and organize

00:09:09.050 --> 00:09:12.350
your pinned repositories that
appear at the top of your page.

00:09:12.350 --> 00:09:15.170
If you've done a solid job on
the resume and cover materials

00:09:15.170 --> 00:09:17.210
that we discussed,
it's a virtual lock

00:09:17.210 --> 00:09:19.640
that your GitHub profile
will be looked at.

00:09:19.640 --> 00:09:22.010
Put good code that
you're proud of there.

00:09:22.010 --> 00:09:24.680
If it doesn't yet have good
documentation or comments,

00:09:24.680 --> 00:09:26.210
add them now.

00:09:26.210 --> 00:09:27.800
Yes the very
careful reader might

00:09:27.800 --> 00:09:29.960
be able to tell that you
added them after the fact,

00:09:29.960 --> 00:09:31.822
but I claim that this
is not a big deal.

00:09:31.822 --> 00:09:33.530
Adding them will show
that you understand

00:09:33.530 --> 00:09:35.240
the importance of
well documented code

00:09:35.240 --> 00:09:38.780
and that you can produce it.

00:09:38.780 --> 00:09:40.310
Taking this all up a notch.

00:09:40.310 --> 00:09:44.900
Here's our friend Hadley's
landing page at his website.

00:09:44.900 --> 00:09:46.560
Looks awesome.

00:09:46.560 --> 00:09:50.680
The great news is that
you too can be Hadley.

00:09:50.680 --> 00:09:54.040
This is made with Sean Kross'
excellent postcard package

00:09:54.040 --> 00:09:56.050
postcards package.

00:09:56.050 --> 00:09:58.450
Hadley used the trestles
template, but there are other

00:09:58.450 --> 00:10:01.540
great looking options here if
you want to branch out a bit.

00:10:01.540 --> 00:10:03.335
Getting started
is made very easy

00:10:03.335 --> 00:10:05.835
with the help of the package
vignette, which is linked here.

00:10:09.800 --> 00:10:11.480
Now here in expert mode.

00:10:11.480 --> 00:10:13.550
You can develop a
full blown website

00:10:13.550 --> 00:10:16.940
with a blog using RStudio,
like Alison Hill has been

00:10:16.940 --> 00:10:19.340
doing elegantly for years now.

00:10:19.340 --> 00:10:22.580
Her most recent iteration uses
Hugo Apero through blogdown,

00:10:22.580 --> 00:10:24.110
which she will
carefully walk you

00:10:24.110 --> 00:10:26.970
through in detail in the
linked recording of her

00:10:26.970 --> 00:10:30.532
R Ladies talk from last year.

00:10:30.532 --> 00:10:32.240
blogdown with Hugo
is one way to do it,

00:10:32.240 --> 00:10:35.810
but other popular options
include distill and Quarto.

00:10:35.810 --> 00:10:37.910
Danielle Navarro has
superb examples

00:10:37.910 --> 00:10:40.040
of both in the form
of a quarto blog

00:10:40.040 --> 00:10:42.140
that was migrated from distill.

00:10:42.140 --> 00:10:43.820
She describes each
of those formats

00:10:43.820 --> 00:10:46.285
and how you develop
in each at her blog

00:10:46.285 --> 00:10:47.410
post, which is linked here.

00:10:50.590 --> 00:10:52.900
When it comes time to publish
your content on the web,

00:10:52.900 --> 00:10:55.600
don't forget that you can
use free services from GitHub

00:10:55.600 --> 00:10:57.438
pages and Netlify.

00:10:57.438 --> 00:10:59.980
There's no need to purchase a
dedicated domain name if you're

00:10:59.980 --> 00:11:01.100
not ready for that yet.

00:11:04.980 --> 00:11:06.750
Overlaying all the
topics we discussed

00:11:06.750 --> 00:11:08.880
is the incredible R community.

00:11:08.880 --> 00:11:11.670
If you ever need support,
want to discover who is hiring,

00:11:11.670 --> 00:11:14.140
or generally get connected
with amazing people,

00:11:14.140 --> 00:11:16.660
here are some directions
I would suggest.

00:11:16.660 --> 00:11:18.840
I'll link from this page.

00:11:18.840 --> 00:11:19.950
In order,

00:11:19.950 --> 00:11:22.260
Rachael Dempsey runs
a virtual weekly

00:11:22.260 --> 00:11:25.140
RStudio data science hangout
with data science leaders

00:11:25.140 --> 00:11:26.610
and community members.

00:11:26.610 --> 00:11:28.200
This informal
get together

00:11:28.200 --> 00:11:29.700
is an excellent way
to stay in touch

00:11:29.700 --> 00:11:33.450
and find out what's new in
the R and data science world.

00:11:33.450 --> 00:11:35.490
Tighty Tuesday is
both a podcast run

00:11:35.490 --> 00:11:38.520
by Jon Harmon and a highly
engaged weekly community

00:11:38.520 --> 00:11:40.410
activity run by Thomas Mock.

00:11:40.410 --> 00:11:42.000
It's a great way to
hone your skills,

00:11:42.000 --> 00:11:46.360
show off your data science
wizardry, and learn from others.

00:11:46.360 --> 00:11:50.140
Tidy Tuesdays is one component
of a larger, very active R

00:11:50.140 --> 00:11:51.880
for Data Science community.

00:11:51.880 --> 00:11:53.650
This community
runs a Slack server

00:11:53.650 --> 00:11:56.320
where you can ask and answer
technical questions or just

00:11:56.320 --> 00:11:58.300
chat about data
science in general.

00:11:58.300 --> 00:12:00.790
One very helpful channel
there is a jobs hiring

00:12:00.790 --> 00:12:04.680
board where you'll see many
R specific jobs posted.

00:12:04.680 --> 00:12:06.840
R user groups or
so-called RUGs

00:12:06.840 --> 00:12:08.680
can be found nearly
anywhere in the world

00:12:08.680 --> 00:12:10.870
these days. If there
isn't one close to you,

00:12:10.870 --> 00:12:12.060
consider starting one.

00:12:12.060 --> 00:12:14.280
The R Consortium
sponsors many of these

00:12:14.280 --> 00:12:15.840
and will help you get started.

00:12:15.840 --> 00:12:17.400
Back when I was in
Tampa, I started

00:12:17.400 --> 00:12:18.900
one of these which
met monthly and I

00:12:18.900 --> 00:12:22.050
met friends which I'm very
close with to this day.

00:12:22.050 --> 00:12:24.570
Many job related connections
tend to happen at these events

00:12:24.570 --> 00:12:26.610
as well.

00:12:26.610 --> 00:12:28.710
In the same vein,
R Ladies Global

00:12:28.710 --> 00:12:30.810
is a worldwide
organization that promotes

00:12:30.810 --> 00:12:32.590
diversity in the R community.

00:12:32.590 --> 00:12:34.398
Much like the RUG meetups,

00:12:34.398 --> 00:12:36.690
you can probably find one of
these running in your area

00:12:36.690 --> 00:12:38.970
and you should
definitely check it out.

00:12:38.970 --> 00:12:42.570
Lastly, the RStudio community
has forums and job boards that

00:12:42.570 --> 00:12:44.790
range from the technical
to community building

00:12:44.790 --> 00:12:46.360
and is a great
place to hang out.

00:12:49.050 --> 00:12:50.850
Final resource I'm
going to leave you with

00:12:50.850 --> 00:12:53.010
is a compilation of
all the resources

00:12:53.010 --> 00:12:55.660
we've discussed and more.

00:12:55.660 --> 00:12:58.810
WTF-AITA.com is
a place where you

00:12:58.810 --> 00:13:03.115
can find these slides and
links to the tools therein.

00:13:03.115 --> 00:13:04.740
But this talk certainly
only represents

00:13:04.740 --> 00:13:07.860
a slice of a much larger set
of guidance and resources

00:13:07.860 --> 00:13:10.020
around industry job seeking.

00:13:10.020 --> 00:13:12.150
Many of you in RStudio
community have already

00:13:12.150 --> 00:13:13.950
traveled this road,
and if there are things

00:13:13.950 --> 00:13:15.700
you'd like to see
represented on the site,

00:13:15.700 --> 00:13:18.780
please do feel free to submit
an issue or a pull request

00:13:18.780 --> 00:13:20.580
at the GitHub repository.

00:13:20.580 --> 00:13:22.440
It's my intent that
the site organically

00:13:22.440 --> 00:13:26.230
grow as a community
owned utility over time.

00:13:26.230 --> 00:13:28.085
With that crowdsourcing
request in mind,

00:13:28.085 --> 00:13:30.460
I'll now hand the discussion
off to the excellent RStudio

00:13:30.460 --> 00:13:31.630
community.

00:13:31.630 --> 00:13:33.370
Thanks so much for
having me at conf,

00:13:33.370 --> 00:13:35.785
and I look forward to seeing
you in the Discord hallways

00:13:35.785 --> 00:13:37.660
and answering any
questions that you may have

00:13:37.660 --> 00:13:40.140
in this particular session.

